---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Why Rust — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="All Rust, or a Rust engine with a web or Swift UI on top? Here's what I weighed, what I gave up, and the one argument that actually decided it."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-06-28">June 28, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Why Rust</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          All Rust, or a Rust engine with a web or Swift UI on top? Here's what
          I weighed, what I gave up, and the one argument that actually decided
          it.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 6 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          I had to choose between building the whole app in Rust or splitting
          it, with a fast Rust engine and a nicer UI in React or Swift. I went
          all Rust, and I gave up a good-looking interface and proper
          accessibility to do it.
        </p>
        <p>
          A photo editor has to answer every mouse movement in real time, and that a bug living in the gap between two languages is exactly the bug I'd end up debugging by hand at midnight.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Write the trade-offs down before you pick.</strong> Listing
          what each option gains and loses forced me to notice what I didn't
          know, and I went and answered those questions first instead of
          letting them ambush me later.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pick a design you can step into.</strong> Agents write nearly
          all of this code, and they're good at it right up until they aren't.
          When one starts fixing symptom after symptom, I need an architecture
          simple enough that I can debug it reliably.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Think a couple of steps ahead, not ten.</strong> Swift would
          have been a perfectly good choice for a Mac-only app. Adding support in the browser ruled it out immediately.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep the core away from the UI.</strong> Even with one
          language, the editing logic lives in its own module.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>A language that reviews you as you type</h2>
      <p>
        Rust is a genuinely interesting language to work in. You get roughly
        the power of C++, and you also get a strict reviewer sitting next to
        you who stops at anything that looks off.
      </p>
      <p>
        That alone doesn't make it right for a photo editor, though. Before I
        wrote much code I had a pile of questions I couldn't answer.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>Is Rust a sensible choice for this at all?</li>
        <li>
          Should I write only the critical piece, the image engine, in Rust and
          build the UI with something like React?
        </li>
        <li>Or should I build the whole thing in Rust?</li>
        <li>Does Rust even have a UI library worth using?</li>
        <li>
          Or should I write the UI in Swift so it feels like a proper Mac app?
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Two more things were nagging at me. I eventually wanted a browser
        version, so I could edit the same way without using my own Mac. I
        had a feeling that I might want to support Windows and Linux one day as well.
      </p>

      <p>It came down to two shapes for the app.</p>

      <figure class="lp-arch">
        <div class="lp-arch-panel">
          <p class="lp-arch-label">Option 1 &middot; All Rust</p>
          <div class="lp-arch-process">
            <span class="lp-arch-process-name">One Rust app</span>
            <div class="lp-arch-box">UI module</div>
            <div class="lp-arch-link">function calls</div>
            <div class="lp-arch-box lp-arch-box-core">Core engine</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-arch-panel">
          <p class="lp-arch-label">Option 2 &middot; Split</p>
          <div class="lp-arch-process">
            <span class="lp-arch-process-name">Electron</span>
            <div class="lp-arch-box">React UI</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lp-arch-link lp-arch-link-cross">
            events &darr;&ensp;pixels &uarr;
          </div>
          <div class="lp-arch-process">
            <span class="lp-arch-process-name">Rust</span>
            <div class="lp-arch-box lp-arch-box-core">Core engine</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          With all Rust, the UI calls the engine directly. With the split, every slider drag and every preview crosses between two runtimes.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>What each one would cost me</h2>
      <p>
        Going all Rust, I'd get one language for the whole app. No image data
        copied between runtimes, no user events marshalled across a bridge,
        better performance, fewer dependencies and a simpler thing to ship.
      </p>
      <p>
        What I'd lose is real too. Rust has no UI toolkit of its own. The community libraries work, but nothing I build with them will look as sharp as a well-styled web page or a native Mac app. Browsers also hand you good accessibility for free, and Rust UI libraries don't come close yet.
      </p>
      <p>
        Splitting it would give me the best of both ecosystems and total
        control over the look, since with CSS I could fuss over every pixel.
        The cost is keeping two systems in sync.
      </p>

      <h2>What about Swift?</h2>
      <p>
        A Swift UI would feel the most at home on a Mac, and I did want that.
        But it has the same split as option two, with Swift talking to a Rust
        engine, and Swift doesn't come with me to the browser. A WASM version
        would need a second UI written from scratch.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also ties me to Apple. Windows or Linux would need yet another UI.
        The Rust UI libraries and Electron both already run on all three.
      </p>

      <h2>Side by side</h2>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">All Rust</th>
              <th scope="col">Rust + React</th>
              <th scope="col">Rust + Swift</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Languages</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">1</td>
              <td>2</td>
              <td>2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Engine and UI to keep in sync</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">No</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>Yes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Extra runtime to ship</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">None</td>
              <td>Electron</td>
              <td>None</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Look</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Plain</td>
              <td>Anything CSS can do</td>
              <td>Native Mac</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Accessibility</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Limited</td>
              <td>Browser-grade</td>
              <td>Native Mac</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Browser (WASM) version</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Same code</td>
              <td>Engine to WASM, UI as is</td>
              <td>Needs a second UI</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Windows and Linux</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Same code</td>
              <td>Same code</td>
              <td>Needs another UI</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        In a web app a little latency is expected and forgiven. You click a
        button, you wait a beat, the server answers. In a photo editor, drag,
        zoom and pan are continuous. Every single mouse movement has to reach
        the engine and come back as a new image, sixty times a second, with no
        lag you can feel.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 220" role="img" aria-label="One slider drag, sixty times a second. With all Rust, the slider moves, an edit function runs and new pixels appear, with no runtime boundary. With a Rust engine and a web UI, the same drag crosses a runtime boundary on the way into the engine and again on the way back, twice per frame.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">ONE SLIDER DRAG, 60 TIMES A SECOND</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="24" width="640" height="88" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="44">All Rust</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="56" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="76" text-anchor="middle">Slider moves</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="192" y1="71" x2="214" y2="71"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M214 66 L224 71 L214 76 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="56" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="76" text-anchor="middle">Edit function</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="400" y1="71" x2="422" y2="71"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M422 66 L432 71 L422 76 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="432" y="56" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="76" text-anchor="middle">New pixels</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="16" y="104">No runtime boundary. It is a function call.</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="124" width="640" height="88" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="16" y="144">Rust engine, web UI</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="16" y="156" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="104" y="176" text-anchor="middle">Slider moves (JS)</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x1="208" y1="152" x2="208" y2="190" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></line>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="224" y="156" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="176" text-anchor="middle">Engine (Rust)</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x1="416" y1="152" x2="416" y2="190" stroke-dasharray="3 4"></line>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="432" y="156" width="176" height="30" rx="5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="520" y="176" text-anchor="middle">New pixels (JS)</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="16" y="204">Two boundary crossings per frame, 120 a second.</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The split version isn't slow because Rust got slower. It's slow
          because the same trip now has two border checkpoints on it.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Keeping two systems agreeing with each other at that rate is much
        harder than answering the occasional request. And when they disagree,
        you get the worst kind of bug.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="lp-pullquote">
        When the two sides disagree, the bug doesn't live in either one. It
        lives in the gap between them.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Why that mattered more than usual for me</h2>
      <p>
        This is where the choice stopped being a normal architecture debate.
      </p>
      <p>
        I didn't write most of this code. Coding agents did, and I've found
        they're strong inside one language and one mental model, and much
        weaker at a subtle mismatch between two systems written in different
        languages.
      </p>
      <p>
        I wanted a design that lets me able to step in and find a problem myself when the agents get stuck. One language and one
        runtime means there's no gap for that class of bug to hide in.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second reason was plain performance. I want this app to be fast and
        to use no more memory than it needs, and with everything in Rust
        there's no second runtime to load and no copies of the image handed
        back and forth.
      </p>

      <h2>Leaving myself a way out</h2>
      <p>
        Even with one language, I keep the UI logic and the core photo editing
        in separate modules. If I change my mind in a year and want a different
        interface, most of the core comes with me untouched.
      </p>
      <p>
        I also clean up sloppy generated code as it arrives rather than letting
        it pile up, which is the same instinct. Both are about keeping the
        thing small enough that I can still hold it in my head.
      </p>


